    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 8).**\n\n**Symptom.** Hypergumbo's `profile.dev_frameworks` on its own repo reports:\n\n```json\n\"dev_frameworks\": [\"pytest\", \"pytorch\", \"transformers\"]\n```\n\nHypergumbo has no PyTorch dependency and no Transformers dependency. The only real deps are `pytest` (correct) and `sentence-transformers` (which transitively depends on PyTorch but is not the same package).\n\n**Root cause locations.**\n\nThe substring matches:\n\n1. `pyproject.toml:9` — `\"torch: tests requiring PyTorch (deselect in CI with -m 'not torch')\"` — pytest marker name. The `\\btorch\\b` regex matches the marker key here.\n\n2. `packages/hypergumbo-core/pyproject.toml:46` — `\"sentence-transformers~=5.2.2\"`. The `\\btransformers\\b` regex matches across the `-` boundary in `sentence-transformers`.\n\nThe detector at `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/profile.py:868-889`:\n\n```python\ndef _detect_python_frameworks(repo_root: Path) -> list[str]:\n    content = \"\"\n    content += _read_all_manifest_files(repo_root, \"pyproject.toml\")\n    content += _read_all_manifest_files(repo_root, \"requirements.txt\")\n    content += _read_all_manifest_files(repo_root, \"setup.py\")\n    content += _read_all_manifest_files(repo_root, \"Pipfile\")\n\n    for framework, patterns in PYTHON_FRAMEWORKS.items():\n        for pattern in patterns:\n            if _manifest_has_package(content, pattern):\n                detected.append(framework)\n                break\n    return detected\n```\n\nreads the **raw concatenated text** of every manifest and runs `_manifest_has_package` (regex word-boundary substring match) against framework names. This cannot distinguish:\n- A real dependency from a pytest marker name\n- A real dependency from a comment\n- One package from a package whose name happens to contain the target as a substring\n\n**Generalization — this is structural, not Python-specific.**\n\n`profile.py` has 18 framework detectors. Two (`_detect_js_frameworks`, `_detect_php_frameworks`) parse the manifest as JSON and inspect specific keys (`dependencies`/`devDependencies`/`require`) — these are SAFE.\n\nThe other **16 detectors all call `_manifest_has_package`** on raw manifest text:\n\n| Language | Manifest file(s) | FP risk surface |\n|---|---|---|\n| Python | pyproject.toml, requirements.txt, setup.py, Pipfile | Pytest markers, comments, partial substring (`sentence-transformers` → `transformers`) |\n| Rust | Cargo.toml | Comments, build-system tables, feature names |\n| Go | go.mod | Vanity module paths (`github.com/foo/transformers`) |\n| Java/Kotlin | pom.xml, build.gradle, build.gradle.kts, gradle/*.gradle | XML comments, partial GAV coords (`spring` → `spring-boot-starter-web`) |\n| Swift, Scala, Dart, Ruby, Elixir, Haskell, Clojure, R, Lua, C++, Erlang, F# | manifest text | Same hazards |\n\nAll 16 inherit the FP pattern from the shared `_manifest_has_package` helper.\n\n**Invariant statement.** Framework detection must only report frameworks declared as actual dependencies in manifest files. It must NEVER report on incidental substring matches in comments, marker names, group names, build-system tables, or partial-substring collisions with unrelated packages.\n\n**Severity.** Medium. Misleads agent consumers about the actual stack in use. For hypergumbo specifically, \"this project uses PyTorch and Transformers\" is a meaningful misclassification — it would shape an agent's expectations of model code, GPU usage, training scripts, etc. that don't exist in this repo.\n\n**Fix proposal — per-format structured parsing.**\n\nReplace `_manifest_has_package` calls with format-aware parsers:\n\n| Format | Approach |\n|---|---|\n| TOML (pyproject.toml, Cargo.toml) | `tomllib.loads()` then inspect `[project.dependencies]`, `[project.optional-dependencies]`, `[dependencies]`, `[dev-dependencies]` only |\n| go.mod | Parse `require ( ... )` blocks; ignore comments |\n| pom.xml | XML parse; inspect `<dependency>/<artifactId>` and `<groupId>` only |\n| build.gradle / Gemfile / mix.exs | Regex on `^\\s*(implementation\\|gem\\|\\\\{:[a-z]+, ...)` patterns at line start; ignore comment lines |\n| requirements.txt / Pipfile | Existing simple parsers; strip comments before regex |\n\nRefactor: extract a `parse_manifest_deps(format, content) -> set[str]` helper that returns just the package-name set per manifest. Then framework detection becomes `if pattern in deps:` (already the safe pattern used by JS and PHP detectors).\n\n**Test gap.** Each detector needs a \"FP test\" with a manifest containing a comment/marker/substring-collision case for the framework names it detects. Currently `test_profile.py:363,387` actually DEMONSTRATES this bug by writing `\"import torch\\n\"` and `\"from transformers import pipeline\\n\"` to temp files — those tests pass because the detector matches the literal string, which is exactly the bug.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/profile.py:841-865` (`_manifest_has_package` — the shared helper)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/profile.py:868-989` (16 vulnerable detectors)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/profile.py:892-920, 962-989` (JS + PHP, the 2 correct ones — use as templates)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_profile.py:363,387` (tests that pass DUE to the bug)"  # bab9
    fields:  # bab9
      statement: "Framework detection must only report frameworks declared as actual dependencies in manifest files. It must never report on incidental substring matches in comments, marker names, group names, build-system tables, or partial-substring collisions with unrelated packages."  # bab9
      root_cause: "profile.py:_manifest_has_package (line 841) does regex word-boundary substring match on lowercased concatenated raw manifest text. 16 of 18 detectors call this helper (Python, Rust, Go, Java/Kotlin x3, Swift, Scala, Dart, Ruby, Elixir, Haskell, Clojure, R, Lua, C++, Erlang, F#). Only JS and PHP correctly use json.loads + dependency-key inspection. Confirmed FPs on hypergumbo self-analysis: 'pytorch' matches a pytest marker name; 'transformers' matches sentence-transformers via word-boundary regex."  # bab9
